    "question": {
      ":question/id": "21db5eb2-e923-586f-8932-cd27b2738332",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{-8x+2}$ at the point where $x=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=-8x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={5}^{u}\\ln(5),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={5}^{u}\\ln(5)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ & =(-8)\\cdot {5}^{u}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =-8\\cdot {5}^{-8x+2}\\ln(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=0} & =(-8)\\cdot {5}^{-8(0)+2}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =-8\\cdot {5}^{2}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =- 200 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $- 200 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={5}^{2}=25.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(25)&=(- 200 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)})\\left(x-(0)\\right) \\\\ y&=- 200 x \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)} + 25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
