    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a8ecd47b-7bdc-584b-a51e-d4b0bbebabf8",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{3x+3}$ at the point where $x=-1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=3x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={5}^{u}\\ln(5),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={5}^{u}\\ln(5)\\cdot (3) \\\\ & =(3)\\cdot {5}^{u}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =3\\cdot {5}^{3x+3}\\ln(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=-1} & =(3)\\cdot {5}^{3(-1)+3}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =3\\cdot {5}^{0}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =3 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=-1$ is $3 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={5}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1)&=(3 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)})\\left(x-(-1)\\right) \\\\ y&=3 x \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)} + 1 + 3 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
